WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.490
Knitting a proper swatch is the key
to success with your knitting machine.

2
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.050
Follow along as I knit a swatch.

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.040
This is my LK 150 and I'm knitting
stockinette, but the process is the same

4
00:00:14.106 --> 00:00:18.160
for all machines and stitch patterns.

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.200
Here are suggestions
for stitch and row counts.

6
00:00:22.266 --> 00:00:26.120
No matter how you plan to measure your
gauge either using a regular

7
00:00:26.186 --> 00:00:28.370
ruler or gauge rulers.

8
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:34.690
You will be measuring a specific number of
stitches and rows within a larger piece.

9
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.810
Measuring within a larger piece allows
for accurate measurement without

10
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.210
the distortion of the edges.

11
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.600
Here are some suggestions.

12
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:50.720
If you're using gauge rulers
for a blue ruler cast on 30 stitches.

13
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.290
This would be maybe for a bulky machine.

14
00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:01.890
For a green ruler cast on 60
stitches for a standard machine.

15
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:08.040
For the yellow ruler cast on 50 stitches
for a mid gauge machine.

16
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.960
Of course, you could cast on a 100
stitches and knit a 100 rows as long as

17
00:01:13.026 --> 00:01:18.120
you have more stitches and rows
than you're going to be measuring.

18
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.530
Use the cast on method of your choice.

19
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.090
Just get some stitches on the machine.

20
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:32.280
I pulled every other needle and worked
an e wrap cast on over every other needle.

21
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.610
Next, thread the carriage.

22
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.290
Wiggle and pull to confirm
that it's threaded correctly.

23
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.000
The yarn is not hung up
and it will flow smoothly.

24
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.890
When you are sure that the carriage is
threaded correctly

25
00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:07.200
pull down on the working yarn
in the back to take up any slack.

26
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:11.200
You can hang your cast on comb now.

27
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:19.240
I knit 1 row first and then added
my cast on comb and a single weight.

28
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.730
Knit a few rows and end
with the carriage on the right.

29
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:32.360
Remove the working yarn from the carriage,
but don't break it or cut it. Just

30
00:02:32.426 --> 00:02:35.800
loop it over the edge of the machine.

31
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.720
Thread up a contrasting color of similar
weight and wiggle and pull to confirm that

32
00:02:43.786 --> 00:02:46.930
it is threaded in the carriage correctly.

33
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.530
If not, you run the risk of dropping
all the stitches off the machine.

34
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.840
Now I like to hang a clothespin or a chip
clip to add some weight

35
00:02:56.906 --> 00:02:59.480
to the tail of yarn.

36
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.770
Knit 2 rows with the contrasting yarn.

37
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.440
Break that waste yarn
and secure it with the clip.

38
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.690
Using the clip ensures
that the end stitches knit well.

39
00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.130
Set your row counter to 000.

40
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.720
Re thread the main yarn in the carriage,
wiggle and pull to make sure

41
00:03:20.786 --> 00:03:25.440
that that yarn is inserted correctly
and if you need to, reach back

42
00:03:25.506 --> 00:03:29.130
and pull down to take up any slack.

43
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.970
Knit a few rows and place some markers.

44
00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.240
Markers are just a short piece
of contrasting color yarn

45
00:03:36.306 --> 00:03:39.200
created with a slip knot.

46
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.330
Knit to the row where you want the marker.

47
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.490
The markers will isolate the number
of stitches you'll be measuring.

48
00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.490
For example, I'm going
to be measuring 30 stitches.

49
00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:00.410
So I'll place my marker on needles
number 16 left and 16 right.

50
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.410
This isolates the center 30 stitches.

51
00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.410
To place a marker put the loop
of the marker in the hook of the needle.

52
00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.280
Pull out the needle so the stitch falls
behind the latch and then pull

53
00:04:15.346 --> 00:04:17.720
the marker through the stitch.
Stitch.

54
00:04:17.786 --> 00:04:21.810
In other words, manually knit
a stitch with the marker loop.

55
00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.000
Do this on both sides.

56
00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.320
Then continue knitting the number
of rows you'll be measuring.

57
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.930
Remove the main yarn
from the feeder but don't cut it.

58
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.690
Loop it around the end of your machine.

59
00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:46.010
Then thread up the contrasting
yarn again and knit 2 rows.

60
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.250
Remember to wiggle and pull to make
sure the carriage is threaded correctly.

61
00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.600
We don't want everything
dropping off the machine.

62
00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:05.010
Knit 2 marker rows with the waste
yarn and break that contrasting color.

63
00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:11.800
Re thread the main yarn,
wiggle and pull and knit 2 or 3 rows.

64
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:24.690
Next, we'll mark our swatch
with the number of our carriage tension.

65
00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:29.320
I'm using tension 6
so I'll create 6 eyelets so I have

66
00:05:29.386 --> 00:05:32.130
a permanent record of the tension.

67
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:35.960
To make an eyelet, use your single prong
transfer tool and remove

68
00:05:36.026 --> 00:05:37.730
a stitch to the tool.

69
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.210
Transfer that stitch to its neighbor.

70
00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.010
You'll empty the needle.

71
00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:50.080
Skip a needle and repeat across
for the number of eyelets that you need.

72
00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.650
Be sure to put all those empty
needles back into working position.

73
00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.440
We want to just create some
eyelets but continue knitting.

74
00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.040
On the LK150
and some other machines you can set up

75
00:06:13.106 --> 00:06:18.800
the tension dial to somewhere
between 6 and 7 for example.

76
00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:23.480
I might have used 6.5 or 6+.

77
00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:29.200
In that case, I would put 6 eyelets,
skip a few needles and then

78
00:06:29.266 --> 00:06:32.090
add an additional eyelet.

79
00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.480
Establish a code for yourself,
whatever works, so you can see exactly the

80
00:06:36.546 --> 00:06:39.770
setting if you look a few months later.

81
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.280
After you form all your eyelets please be
sure to make sure all your

82
00:06:43.346 --> 00:06:45.490
needles are in working position.

83
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.770
Knit a few more rows and then bind off.

84
00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.930
I'm using my favorite bind off.

85
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.250
I'm manually knitting that last
row with fairly large loops.

86
00:06:55.320 --> 00:07:01.440
Put the yarn in the hooks of the needles
and manually knit each 1 of the stitches.

87
00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:49.920
To prepare for the bind off, carefully
pull all the needles all the way out.

88
00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.320
I'll pull loop through loop to bind off.

89
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.890
Use whatever bind off method you like.

90
00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:35.120
Swatches are a great opportunity to learn
and practice different bind

91
00:08:35.186 --> 00:08:38.160
off and cast on methods.

92
00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.920
Use the tail of yarn
to finish off the last stitch.

93
00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:56.320
With the swatch off the machine
and the ends secure you can now wash your

94
00:08:56.386 --> 00:09:01.530
swatch, block it and use whatever methods
you'll be using for your finished garment.

95
00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.290
This is called dressing your swatch.

96
00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:08.267
It's a good time to discover
how the yarn behaves.

97
00:09:08.333 --> 00:09:11.570
Now don't measure your
swatch right off the machine.

98
00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:15.560
Let it rest at least
24 hours then dress it.

99
00:09:15.626 --> 00:09:18.930
You might want to let it
rest a little bit again.

100
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:24.370
Your next step is to use a regular ruler
or a gauge ruler to establish your gauge.

101
00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:29.330
I suggest attaching a string tag to record
your machine, tension,

102
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:35.080
gauge, dressing method, and any
other notes about your swatch.

103
00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:40.440
Knitting a proper swatch is the secret
to successful machine knitting.
